slstatus - suckless status
==========================
slstatus is a suckless status monitor for window managers that use WM_NAME
(e.g. dwm) or stdin to fill the status bar.


Features
--------
- Battery percentage/power/state
- CPU usage
- CPU frequency
- CPU iowait
- Custom shell commands
- Date and time
- Disk status (free storage, percentage, total storage and used storage)
- Available entropy
- Username/GID/UID
- Hostname
- IP address (IPv4 and IPv6)
- Kernel version
- Keyboard indicators
- Load average
- Number of files in a directory (hint: Maildir)
- Memory status (free memory, percentage, total memory and used memory)
- Swap status (free swap, percentage, total swap and used swap)
- Temperature
- Uptime
- Volume percentage (OSS/ALSA)
- WiFi signal percentage and ESSID
- Razer Basilisk X HyperSpeed DPI and battery status
- Claude, OpenAI, Grok, and OpenCode Go subscription usage remaining
- Herdr agent states with an animated attention indicator
- Click controls for volume and mouse DPI when used with the clickable-status
  patch in this dwm configuration


Status clicks
-------------
- Volume: left click adds 10%, right click subtracts 10% (clamped to 0-100%)
- Mouse DPI: left click adds 100 DPI, right click subtracts 100 DPI (clamped
  to 100-16000 DPI)
- Date: left click shows a three-month calendar in a small `st` popup

The mouse component writes the active DPI and its single DPI stage together,
so the physical DPI button remains locked to the displayed value.


Requirements
------------
In order to build slstatus you need the Xlib header files and optionally ALSA
for volume percentage. PulseAudio is not supported for various reasons.

- I needed `pulseaudio` and `pusleaudio-alsa` in order to get my headphone volume controls working.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (slstatus is installed into the
/usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install slstatus (if
necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running slstatus
----------------
See the man page for details.


Configuration
-------------
slstatus can be customized by creating a custom config.h and (re)compiling the
source code. This keeps it fast, secure and simple.


Herdr agent status
------------------
The Herdr component reads the local Unix socket API directly and shows a count
for each agent state: `!` blocked, `✓` done, `…` working, `○` idle, and `?`
unknown. Zero counts are omitted.

Blocked and done agents need attention. While either state is present, its `!`
or `✓` icon blinks on and off on each status refresh. The count remains visible
and the blank frame preserves the icon's width, so the rest of the status does
not move. The `○N` idle/parked count remains steadily visible. For example:

    Herdr !1 ✓1 …2 ○1
    Herdr  1  1 …2 ○1

The component hides itself when Herdr is unavailable or has no detected
agents. Pass a socket path in `config.h` to override discovery. With a `NULL`
argument it checks `HERDR_SOCKET_PATH`, then
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/herdr/herdr.sock`, or finally
`$HOME/.config/herdr/herdr.sock`.


AI subscription usage
---------------------
The Claude, OpenAI, Grok, and OpenCode Go components display remaining
subscription capacity as five-cell progress bars with the exact percentage
alongside them, for example `5hr [███▊░] 75%`. A fuller bar means more capacity
remains.

The configured status bar keeps these provider segments hidden. Left-click the
`󰚩` agent segment to show all four providers in a small `st` popup below the
top-right status area. The popup uses the normal `st` font and closes when it
loses focus. Opening it refreshes OpenCode Go once, then shows all four
providers. Run `slstatus -u` to refresh and print the same four-row report in a
terminal.

Left-click the date to show `cal -3` in the same type of popup. DWM places both
popups at the top right before their first visible frame.

OpenAI usage is read from the newest Codex rollout under
`$CODEX_HOME/sessions` (or `~/.codex/sessions`). Codex writes the same rate-limit
snapshot used by its own interface after a response. Because the rollout format
is internal to Codex, an incompatible future format is treated as unavailable.

Grok usage is read from the latest available subscription snapshot in
`$GROK_HOME/logs/unified.jsonl` (or `~/.grok/logs/unified.jsonl`). The Grok CLI
writes its used percentage and weekly reset information to this local log. The
component reads new log data instead of contacting an xAI server.

Claude Code exposes subscription rate limits to its custom status-line command
but does not persist them in its normal stats cache. Install this project's
helper and configure it as the Claude Code status line:

    "statusLine": {
        "type": "command",
        "command": "/usr/local/libexec/slstatus/claude-usage-cache"
    }

The helper requires `jq`, preserves the `[user@host directory]` Claude footer,
and writes only the two percentages and reset times to
`${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/slstatus/claude-usage-v1`.

The Claude, OpenAI, and Grok components hide missing or expired windows. Values
update only when the corresponding CLI receives new usage data, so they can
remain visible while offline until the provider-reported reset time.

OpenCode Go does not provide a public usage API. Opening the AI usage report
starts one helper request. There is no background polling. The helper reads the
rolling, weekly, and monthly windows from the authenticated workspace dashboard
and stores only their percentages and reset times in
`${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/slstatus/opencode-go-usage-v1`.

Create `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/slstatus/opencode-go.json` with mode
`600`:

    {
        "workspaceId": "wrk_...",
        "authCookie": "..."
    }

The workspace ID is in the Go dashboard URL. Get the `auth` cookie value from
the browser developer tools while signed in. Do not commit or share this file.
The cookie grants access to the OpenCode account until it expires.

The helper has a ten-second request limit. It removes its cache after a network,
authentication, configuration, or parsing failure. In these cases, the report
shows OpenCode Go as unavailable and continues to run.
